verdict.explanation:
The defect is real. Line 48 says the main note's Boolean codec is "of this kind" (influence or Fourier structured search) and "ran in seconds per network". Neither part matches the code or the run records.

1) What the codec does. compress() in /home/user/AI-works/compression-generalization/code/cgb/boolean.py:
- estimates influences on 2000 points by flipping each of the n coordinates;
- picks candidate sizes k from influence thresholds, plus k=n;
- for each k, builds a truth table on the top-k coordinates when k<=14;
- also, for each k, distills an MLP student for each width in widths_h=(0,1,2,4,8,16) (2000 steps), then quantization-aware-trains it at each of 3 step sizes rels=(0.5,0.25,0.125) (500 steps each). The width loop stops early only if the float student's disagreement falls below 1e-4.

There is no Fourier or Kushilevitz-Mansour step. The distilled students are the "distillation" heuristic of the next bullet, not structured search, and they are often the chosen description: best_label is an MLP student in 38 of 72 runs in grid_n20.jsonl and 76 of 147 in grid_n50.jsonl.

2) Runtime. code/experiments/boolean_run.py sets torch.set_num_threads(1), and the grid runners also pin OMP/MKL threads to 1. It records secs (the whole run) and secs_train (training f). Recomputing secs - secs_train from results/boolean:
- grid_n20.jsonl (72 runs): 45.0 to 330.0 s, median 104.25 s;
- grid_n50.jsonl (147 runs): 31.8 to 254.1 s, median 117.0 s.

These match the referee's figures. That is about half a minute to five and a half minutes per network, not seconds. One caveat: secs - secs_train also includes computing f's test error and the final certification, not only compress(). So "most of it distillation" is plausible from the code but was not timed separately. Neither the main note nor its results summaries claims a seconds-scale runtime anywhere.
